steps.body:
Older code often uses **field injection**:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired private PaymentClient payments; // discouraged
}
```

Prefer constructor injection:

- Fields can be `final`: the object is never half-built.
- Dependencies are visible in one place. A constructor with eight parameters is an honest sign the class does too much.
- Unit tests just call `new OrderService(fakePayments, fakeRepo)`: no Spring, no reflection (see the Unit Testing course).
- Circular dependencies fail fast at startup instead of hiding.

You can't add `@Service` to a library class like `java.time.Clock`. Instead, write a factory method in a `@Configuration` class:

```java
@Configuration
public class TimeConfig {

    @Bean
    public Clock clock() {
        return Clock.systemUTC();
    }
}
```

The return value becomes a bean (named after the method: `clock`) that any class can request in its constructor. A test can then supply a fixed clock instead of the real one.

