```java
// Simplified sketch of what Boot does for you
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public DataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties props) {
        // build a HikariCP pool from the spring.datasource.* properties
    }
}
```

The conditions you'll meet most:

- `@ConditionalOnClass`: only if a library is on the classpath. Adding a starter flips these on.
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: only if **you** haven't defined a bean of that type. This is how you override Boot.
- `@ConditionalOnProperty`: only if a property has a given value.
- `@ConditionalOnBean`: only if some other bean exists.

Spring injects by **type**. One matching bean: done. Two matching beans: it refuses to guess.

```java
@Component
public class StripeClient implements PaymentClient { ... }

@Component
public class PaypalClient implements PaymentClient { ... }

@Service
public class OrderService {
    public OrderService(PaymentClient payments) { ... }
}
```

Startup fails with: *Parameter 0 of constructor in OrderService required a single bean, but 2 were found*.

- `@Primary` on one implementation: it wins whenever there's ambiguity.
- `@Qualifier` on the injection point: pick a bean by name. A scanned class's default bean name is its simple name with a lowercase first letter (`paypalClient`); a `@Bean` method's is the method name.
- Inject them all: `List<PaymentClient>`, or `Map<String, PaymentClient>` keyed by bean name. Handy for strategy-style code.

```java
@Service
public class RefundService {
    public RefundService(@Qualifier("paypalClient") PaymentClient payments) { ... }
}
```

