Validation doesn't descend into nested objects on its own. Mark them with `@Valid` too:

```java
public record OrderRequest(
        @NotNull @Valid Address shipping,
        @NotEmpty List<@Valid OrderLine> lines) {}
```

Annotations are for the **shape** of the input. Rules that need data, like "this email is already registered", belong in your service, which throws an exception you map to a status code.

```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class ApiExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(BookNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail notFound(BookNotFoundException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
    }
}
```

- `@RestControllerAdvice` applies to every controller; return values become the response body.
- `@ExceptionHandler` names the exception type(s) a method handles.
- `ProblemDetail` models the standard error format of RFC 9457 (which replaced RFC 7807). Spring uses its `status` as the response code and sends `Content-Type: application/problem+json`.

