```java
import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "customers")
public class Customer {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;

    private String name;

    protected Customer() {}        // required by JPA

    public Customer(String email, String name) {
        this.email = email;
        this.name = name;
    }
    // getters, and setters for what may change
}
```

JPA (implemented by Hibernate) maps each instance to a table row. Entities need an `@Id`, a no-argument constructor, and must not be `final`, so they are classes, **not records**. Keep records for your DTOs.

```java
public interface CustomerRepository extends JpaRepository<Customer, Long> {
    Optional<Customer> findByEmail(String email);
    List<Customer> findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String fragment);
    boolean existsByEmail(String email);
}
```

Spring Data generates the implementation at startup (no `@Repository` annotation needed on the interface). You get `save`, `findById` (returns an `Optional`), `findAll`, `deleteById`, `count` and paging for free.

On top of that, **derived queries**: the method name is parsed into a query. `findBy` + property name + optional keywords such as `And`, `Or`, `GreaterThan`, `After`, `Containing`, `IgnoreCase` or `OrderBy`.

