```java
@WebMvcTest(BookController.class)
class BookControllerTest {

    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
    @MockitoBean BookService books;

    @Test
    void returnsBook() throws Exception {
        when(books.find(42L)).thenReturn(new BookResponse(42L, "Dune", "Herbert"));

        mvc.perform(get("/api/books/42"))
           .andExpect(status().isOk())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.title").value("Dune"));
    }
}
```

**MockMvc** pushes fake HTTP requests through the real Spring MVC machinery, with no server. `@MockitoBean` (Spring Framework 6.2+) puts a Mockito mock into the context in place of the real bean; it replaces `@MockBean`, deprecated since Spring Boot 3.4. Field injection is fine in tests.

```java
@DataJpaTest
class CustomerRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired CustomerRepository customers;

    @Test
    void findsByEmail() {
        customers.save(new Customer("ann@example.com", "Ann"));
        assertThat(customers.findByEmail("ann@example.com")).isPresent();
    }
}
```

`@DataJpaTest` sets up JPA and your repositories only, uses an embedded in-memory database by default (if one is on the classpath), and rolls back each test's transaction. To test against your real database engine, pair it with Testcontainers.

`@SpringBootTest` starts everything; add `webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT` to run a real server for true end-to-end calls. Keep these few: they're the slowest.

